Tayammum takes the place of wudu or the full bath when water cannot be used:

- there is no water, or only enough for drinking;
- using water would harm you because of an illness or a wound;
- water is too far away or too dangerous to reach.

Allah says: "If you are ill, or on a journey, or one of you comes from relieving himself, or you have touched women, and you find no water, then seek clean earth and wipe your faces and your hands with it" (5:6).

In the Shafi'i school tayammum is made after the prayer time has come in, and one tayammum is used for one obligatory prayer.

When you stop at the end of an ayah, the last letter loses its short vowel. *Wal-'asri* is read *wal-'asr*.

Three more things happen when stopping:

- a double fathah (ـًا) becomes a long "a";
- the round ta (ة) is read as "h";
- other tanween is dropped.

Small marks above the line tell you where you may breathe:

- **مـ** stop here;
- **لا** do not stop here;
- **ج** you may stop or go on;
- **صلى** going on is better;
- **قلى** stopping is better.

It is always correct to stop at the end of an ayah.

A nikah is a contract, and it needs five things:

- the free consent of the bride and of the groom;
- the bride's guardian (wali);
- two Muslim witnesses;
- the mahr, a gift from the groom that belongs to the bride alone;
- the offer and the acceptance, said clearly in one sitting.

In the Maldives the marriage is also registered at the court. A short sermon and a dua are said before the contract.

Allah made prayer lighter on a journey. In the Shafi'i school these concessions are yours when all of these are true:

- your journey is about **81 km or more one way** (roughly 44 nautical miles);
- the journey is for something lawful;
- you have **left your island** or the edge of your town;
- you will stay at any one place for **less than four full days**. The day you arrive and the day you leave are not counted.

A trip from Male' to a nearby island in the same atoll is usually too short. Male' to Addu, to the far north, or abroad is far enough.

In this app, turn on **I am travelling** on the prayer card. It shows how far you are from home.
